course: medicine
lesson: 24
title: Sugar, teeth and the dentist
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A sticky film called plaque, full of bacteria, grows on your teeth every day; when you eat sugar the bacteria feed on it and make acid that dissolves the hard enamel.
explain: After each sugary snack the acid attack lasts about an hour before saliva repairs the damage, so five sweets nibbled through the afternoon do far more harm than five sweets eaten straight after lunch.
explain: If the attacks win, a hole, a cavity, eats into the tooth, and the dentist has to clean it out and fill it before it reaches the nerve.
explain: Fluoride toothpaste is the best defence: it hardens enamel and mends early damage, which is why you spit after brushing but don't rinse it away.
fact: Bacteria in plaque feed on sugar and make acid, which dissolves enamel and causes tooth decay, a hole called a cavity.
fact: How often you eat sugar matters most for teeth, because each sugary snack starts an acid attack of about an hour.
fact: For a filling the dentist numbs the tooth with a local anaesthetic injection, and the lip can stay numb for a few hours, so you mustn't chew it or eat hot food until feeling returns.
fact: Dentists advise brushing for two minutes twice a day, last thing at night and one other time, with fluoride toothpaste, and spitting out rather than rinsing.
fact: Between about 6 and 12 years old the 20 baby teeth fall out as adult teeth push up underneath, and most children have 28 adult teeth by about 13, with wisdom teeth sometimes later.
fact: Baby teeth matter because they hold the space for adult teeth.
fact: Fissure sealants are thin plastic coatings painted into the grooves of back teeth to keep food and bacteria out, but they don't replace brushing.
fact: Braces slowly move teeth with steady pressure over months or years, and an orthodontist usually decides on them around age 12 or older; they are not only for looks, because straighter teeth can bite better and are easier to clean.
fact: Acid in fizzy drinks and fruit juice wears enamel away even when there is no sugar, which is called erosion, and brushing straight after an acidic drink can rub away the softened enamel, so it's better to wait about an hour.
fact: Floss or small brushes clean between teeth where a toothbrush can't reach, and gums that bleed at first usually settle with regular cleaning.
fact: Tooth decay is the most common reason children in the UK need an operation in hospital, usually to remove teeth.
fact: Many people feel nervous about the dentist, and dentists can agree a stop signal, like raising a hand.
exercise: What makes a hole in a tooth? | Acid from plaque bacteria feeding on sugar | Biting hard food like apples | Brushing too often | Drinking milk
exercise: Which matters most for teeth: how much sugar or how often? | How often | How much, only | Neither, only brushing matters | The colour of the sweets
exercise: How long does an acid attack last after a sugary snack? | About an hour | About a minute | About a week | All day and night
exercise: Why was your lip numb after a filling? | A local anaesthetic blocked the nerves | The filling squashed your lip | The dentist's glove was cold and froze it a bit | You bit it during the filling
exercise: How long should you brush? | Two minutes, twice a day | Ten seconds, once a week | Ten minutes after every snack | Only when teeth look dirty
exercise: What should you do after brushing with fluoride toothpaste? | Spit, don't rinse | Rinse well with water | Drink fruit juice | Swallow a mouthful of paste
exercise: How many baby teeth do children have? | 20 | 32 | 10 | 28
exercise: Why do baby teeth matter if they fall out anyway? | They hold space for adult teeth | They grow back again if they are lost | They turn into wisdom teeth later on | They don't really matter at all, honestly
exercise: What does a fissure sealant do? | Seals the grooves of back teeth against food and bacteria | Whitens teeth | Straightens crooked teeth | Replaces brushing
exercise: How do braces work? | Steady pressure moves teeth over months | They pull teeth straight in just one day at the dentist | They glue teeth together forever | They make teeth grow longer
exercise: Can sugar-free fizzy drinks harm teeth? | Yes, their acid wears enamel | No, only sugar can harm your teeth | Only if they are drunk while warm | Only if you drink them at night
exercise: What does floss do? | Cleans between teeth where a brush can't reach | Makes teeth whiter in a day | Numbs the gums | Replaces brushing
exercise: Your gums bleed when you start flossing. What now? | Keep going gently, it usually settles | Stop flossing for ever, it's doing harm | Floss much harder so the gums toughen up | Rinse with fizzy drinks to stop the bleeding
exercise: What is the most common reason UK children have an operation in hospital? | Tooth decay | Broken arms | Appendicitis | Tonsils
exercise: What can help if you're scared of the dentist? | Agreeing a stop signal and asking questions | Never going again | Eating sweets first | Hiding in the toilet until the appointment is over
exercise: When should sweets be eaten to protect teeth? | At mealtimes, not nibbled all day | Little by little all afternoon long | Just before bed | Every hour on the hour
test: "brushed my teeth right after my coke, so it's fine" — any problem? | Brushing straight after acid can wear enamel, wait an hour | No, brushing straight after is best | Coke has no acid | Only rinsing helps
test: Kid A eats a bag of sweets after lunch. Kid B eats the same bag one by one all afternoon. Whose teeth suffer more? | Kid B, many acid attacks | Kid A, more sugar at once | Both the same | Neither, sweets don't hurt teeth
test: "Baby teeth fall out anyway, why fill 'em?" Best answer? | Decay hurts and baby teeth hold space for adult ones | No point, leave them | So the tooth fairy pays more | Baby teeth turn into adult ones
test: Cartoon dentist pulls teeth with pliers while the kid screams. What's real? | Dentists numb the area and agree a stop signal | Dentists never numb anything | Dentists always pull teeth out, even healthy ones | Kids must stay silent
test: Your lip's still numb after a filling and you're starving. What's the risk with hot pizza? | You could bite or burn your lip without feeling it | None, numb lips can't be hurt | The filling will melt | The numbness makes hot food taste much better than usual
test: What would happen if you never brushed for a year? | Plaque would build up and decay and gum disease would likely follow | Teeth would clean themselves | Teeth would turn stronger | Nothing, saliva does it all
test: "Braces are just for looks" — fair? | No, they can also help the bite and cleaning | Yes, only for looks | Yes, they do nothing else | No, they're really there to make teeth grow longer
test: Rinsing with water after brushing — good idea? | No, it washes the fluoride away | Yes, rinse twice with lots of water | Yes, always rinse really well after | Only if you used a lot of toothpaste
test: Mo, 7, has a gap where a tooth fell out. What's growing? | An adult tooth that was underneath | Another baby tooth, just like the old one | A wisdom tooth, the very first of them | Nothing, the gap will stay there for good
test: Which drink between meals is kindest to teeth? | Water | Orange juice | Cola | Sports drink
test: The dentist painted my back teeth, so I can skip brushing them, yeah? | No, sealants help but don't replace brushing | Yes, they're sealed forever | Yes, but only at weekends | No, because the paint wears off in a single day
exam: Identify the principal process by which dental caries is initiated. | Acid produced by plaque bacteria from dietary sugar dissolves the enamel | Mechanical abrasion from the consumption of firm fruit | Excessive brushing with fluoride preparations | The consumption of milk
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the frequency of sugar consumption. | Frequent consumption between meals is more injurious than consumption at mealtimes | The total quantity alone determines injury | Frequency is of no consequence whatsoever | Sugar exerts no influence upon the teeth
exam: Ascertain the conduct recommended subsequent to brushing with fluoride toothpaste. | Expectoration without rinsing | Thorough rinsing with water | Immediate consumption of fruit juice | Swallowing of the residue
exam: Determine the number of deciduous teeth, hereinafter baby teeth, ordinarily possessed by a child. | Twenty | Thirty-two | Twenty-eight | Ten
